  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 335 AM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nMid-level water vapor imagery this morning shows ridging across the \ncentral Plains just ahead of a negitively-tilted trough across the \nGreat Basin and central Rockies. This shortwave has led to ample \nprecipitation across much of the Rockies and desert southwest with \nupper-level moisture filtering into the high Plains. Over the next \nfew days, the upper-level ridge will remain overhead keeping dry and \nwarm conditions across eastern Kansas. Expect highs each afternoon \nthrough Sunday to top out in the upper 70s with lows each morning in \nthe mid 50s. \n\nChanges in the warm and dry weather builds in Sunday night into \nMonday as the western trough begins to eject off the Colorado \nRockies. Mid-level lift paired with isentropic upglide should \nincrease across much of western and central Kansas leading to \nwidespread precipitation in those areas into Monday morning. Best \nchances to see precipitation will remain across far central Kansas \n(30-50%) with chances decreasing as you head towards far eastern \nKansas. A weak frontal boundary passage Tuesday morning should bring \nan end to precipitation chances as the main upper low passes off to \nour north and east Tuesday into Wednesday. Temperatures behind this \nweak front should return to near-average for late October. By the \nlater half of the work week, long-range guidance begins to bring in \na few more troughs from the west coast. There is still some timing \nand location discrepancies between models at this time, but general \nconsensus is for a more active pattern by late next week that should \nreturn low-end PoPs to eastern Kansas.",
